Home Assistant Alexa Integration Intermittent Disconnections

Overview
Home Assistant's alexa_devices (aioamazondevices) integration was experiencing periodic disconnections roughly every 30 minutes. Rather than an Amazon authentication or token expiration issue, the root cause was an upstream WAN DHCP lease renewal on the main router briefly interrupting outbound routing.
Environment
Host: Raspberry Pi 5 (raspi1) running Home Assistant in Docker (network_mode: host)
Main Router: TP-Link Archer BE550 (192.168.1.1)
ISP Gateway: CH7465ONE (192.168.0.1, double-NAT; Bridge Mode unavailable in UI)
Symptoms
Home Assistant logs showed OSError 113 (EHOSTUNREACH / No route to host) and OSError 101 (ENETUNREACH / Network unreachable) at ~30-minute intervals

Diagnostics (Ruled Out)
Physical Link (eth0): dmesg -T | grep -iE "link|eth0|wlan0|carrier|reset" showed zero physical interface drops.
IPv6 Routing: sudo docker exec homeassistant curl -6 -I [https://api.amazon.com](https://api.amazon.com) returned no AAAA records; curl -4 succeeded.
Docker Networking: Confirmed homeassistant uses host network mode (docker inspect -f '{{.HostConfig.NetworkMode}}' homeassistant).
Host OS / DHCP: sudo journalctl -u NetworkManager -u dhcpcd showed no local lease drops or interface resets on raspi1.
Root Cause
Checking Advanced > System > System Log on the Archer BE550 revealed DHCPC renewal events matching the exact timestamps of the Home Assistant errors

Fix
Changing the Archer BE550's IP address from dynamic to static
